      "text": "You are comparing two texts written in response to the same task. Decide which one reads MORE like unedited, default output from an AI language model.\n\nJudge ONLY the presence of the tells listed below. Do not judge argument quality, factual accuracy, length, formatting preference, or whether you agree with the content. A longer, better-argued or more polished text is not more human. Pick the text with MORE tells.\n\nTELLS OF UNEDITED AI PROSE (judge on these only)\n\nStructure\n1. Negative parallelism used as a device: \"It's not X. It's Y.\" / \"not just X, but Y\" / \"isn't about X, it's about Y\", especially more than once.\n2. Signposting: the text announces what it is about to do (\"Let's break this down\", \"Here's the thing\", \"Here's why\", \"The bottom line\", \"The takeaway\").\n3. Self-applause: \"Let that sink in\", \"This changes everything\", \"Read that again\", \"Make no mistake\".\n4. Recap endings: a closing paragraph that restates the body (\"In conclusion\", \"Ultimately\", \"At the end of the day\", \"In short\").\n5. Bold-label paragraph openers (\"**The problem.** ...\") and headers, bullets or numbered lists where prose was asked for.\n6. Triads everywhere: lists of exactly three items or three adjectives, sentence after sentence.\n7. Participial tails: sentences ending in \", making it ...\", \", ensuring that ...\", \", allowing teams to ...\".\n8. Invented labels for ordinary observations (\"I call this the Clarity Flywheel\").\n9. Uniform rhythm: every sentence about the same length, or relentless short punchy fragments. Real writing has one sentence that runs on and one short one.\n10. Rhetorical question followed by its own answer (\"The result? Nobody used it.\").\n\nWords\n11. Overused vocabulary: delve, tapestry, myriad, underscore, seamless, robust, foster, elevate, unlock, testament, landscape, navigate, crucial, pivotal, leverage, harness, realm, embark, journey, transformative, paradigm, ever-evolving, multifaceted, holistic, resonate, empower, unleash, beacon, intricate, nuanced, vibrant, meticulous, streamline, game-changer, cutting-edge, actionable, supercharge, north star, move the needle, \"in today's fast-paced world\", \"it's worth noting\", \"plays a crucial role\".\n12. Filler transitions at sentence start: However, Additionally, Furthermore, Moreover, That said, Ultimately, Importantly, Indeed, Of course.\n13. Em dashes used as the default joint between clauses.\n14. Artificial enthusiasm: \"thrilled to announce\", \"excited to share\", \"we've got you covered\", \"say goodbye to\", \"buckle up\".\n\nReader\n15. Performing for an imagined crowd rather than talking to one person; nothing specific (no number, name, date, or concrete detail) for several sentences in a row.\n16. Generic or manufactured anecdotes: a story with no specific details that could only be true of one person.\n17. Every point lands, every ending uplifts; the author is never irritated, uncertain, or wrong about anything.\n18. Explaining what the reader could infer from the previous sentence (\"what this means is ...\").\n\n\nTASK GIVEN TO BOTH WRITERS:\nWrite an essay of up to 500 words on why most product roadmaps fail.\n\n=== TEXT A ===\n# Why Most Product Roadmaps Fail\n\nNearly every product organization produces a roadmap, yet remarkably few products follow one to success. The failure is rarely a matter of poor planning discipline. It is structural: the conventional roadmap rests on assumptions that guarantee its own obsolescence.\n\nThe first and deepest flaw is that roadmaps describe outputs when products succeed on outcomes. A typical roadmap is a list of features attached to dates. But features are solutions, and solutions are hypotheses about what customers need. By committing to a solution a year in advance, a team forecloses the learning that would produce a better one. The roadmap answers \"what will we build?\" while never asking \"will it work?\" Teams that ship the roadmap faithfully can still ship products nobody wants — they executed the plan and ignored the evidence.\n\nThe second flaw is false precision. Software development involves irreducible uncertainty; attaching dates to uncertain work converts a plan into a promise. Once the roadmap becomes an accountability instrument, behavior distorts: estimates get padded, scope gets cut to hit deadlines, and quality erodes. Or the dates are missed and trust erodes instead. Either way, the roadmap fails socially before it fails technically.\n\nThird, roadmaps are static artifacts in dynamic environments. Markets shift, competitors move, user research confounds expectations. A roadmap drafted in an annual planning cycle and rarely revisited is a snapshot mistaken for a moving picture. When reality diverges from the document, the roadmap is quietly abandoned rather than revised, and with it goes whatever strategic coherence it carried.\n\nFourth, many roadmaps are political documents first and strategic ones second. They serve as negotiation instruments: a slot for the loudest salesperson's client, a line item for the executive with the strongest opinion, a gesture to appease an anxious board. Items accrete without argument about trade-offs. The result is not a strategy expressed as a sequence but a queue of promises — and a queue is not a direction.\n\nUnderlying all of this is a deeper misunderstanding: the belief that the plan is the product of planning. The value of planning lies in the thinking it forces — what we believe, why we believe it, what evidence would change our minds. The document is merely the residue. Organizations that treat the residue as sacred lose the agility the thinking was meant to provide.\n\nRoadmaps that work look different. They are anchored in outcomes — problems to solve, metrics to move — rather than feature lists. They express direction and confidence rather than precision: what we are doing now, what is likely next, what might come later. They are living documents, revised as learning accumulates rather than defended as commitments. And every item traces back to an articulated strategy, so that \"no\" is as visible as \"yes.\"\n\nMost roadmaps fail not because teams plan, but because they plan the wrong things: features instead of outcomes, dates instead of direction, documents instead of decisions. The remedy is not better forecasting but honesty about uncertainty — a roadmap built to change its mind.\n=== END OF TEXT A ===\n\n=== TEXT B ===\n**Why Most Product Roadmaps Fail**\n\nA product roadmap is supposed to be a compass. In practice, it usually becomes a contract—one that nobody intended to sign but everyone feels bound by. This tension between what a roadmap is meant to do and what it actually does explains why so many of them collapse under their own weight.\n\nThe first failure is one of certainty. Roadmaps are typically built as sequences of features with dates attached, presented with a confidence that the underlying knowledge does not justify. Teams commit to shipping Feature X in Q3 before anyone has validated that customers want Feature X, that it can be built in the time estimated, or that the market will still care by Q3. The document projects precision onto a fundamentally uncertain future. When reality diverges—as it always does—the roadmap either becomes a source of missed deadlines and eroded trust, or it is quietly abandoned. Either way, it has stopped functioning as a guide.\n\nThe second failure is that roadmaps are often lists of outputs rather than expressions of strategy. A good roadmap should answer *why*: what problem are we solving, for whom, and what will be different if we succeed? Most roadmaps instead answer *what*: a login redesign, a reporting dashboard, an integration with a popular tool. Stripped of intent, these items cannot be reprioritized intelligently. When a competitor moves or a customer churns, the team has no framework for deciding what to cut, because the roadmap never articulated what mattered most. It becomes a queue, and queues do not adapt.\n\nThird, roadmaps fail because they serve too many masters. Sales wants commitments to close deals. Executives want a narrative for the board. Engineering wants scope clarity. Customers want to know when their request will land. A single document straining to satisfy all of these becomes bloated, hedged, and ultimately incoherent. Features get added to placate a stakeholder rather than to advance the product, and the roadmap fills with items that exist for political rather than strategic reasons. Saying yes is easy; the roadmap becomes the graveyard of unspoken noes.\n\nFourth, roadmaps are rarely maintained as living documents. They are produced during planning season, circulated with fanfare, and then left to decay. Six months later, the team is working on things that never appeared on it, while items still listed have been silently deprioritized. The roadmap and the reality drift apart until the document is fiction. A roadmap that is not revisited regularly is not a plan—it is an artifact.\n\nUnderlying all of these is a deeper confusion: treating the roadmap as a promise rather than a hypothesis. The best product organizations frame their roadmaps around outcomes, express confidence honestly, keep near-term items specific and far-term items deliberately vague, and revise openly as they learn. They understand that the roadmap's value lies not in its accuracy but in the conversations it forces about priorities and trade-offs.\n\nMost roadmaps fail, then, not because planning is futile, but because they mistake the plan for the goal. A roadmap that cannot change is not a tool for navigation. It is a monument to what a team once believed.\n=== END OF TEXT B ===\n\nAnswer with a single JSON object and nothing else:\n{\"more_ai\": \"A\" or \"B\", \"confidence\": 1-5, \"tells_A\": [\"short tell\", ...], \"tells_B\": [\"short tell\", ...]}\n"
